    {
      "id": "b9e6qg0bFRYiNbRwTbVi6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 240.98286055043985,
      "y": 3369.275241946857,
      "width": 1286.9788818359375,
      "height": 1325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 414365270,
      "version": 1440,
      "versionNonce": 161954854,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781634444077,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Response format\n{\n  \"post\": {\n    \"id\": \"124\",\n    \"authorId\": \"456\",\n    \"body\": { \"text\": \"Hello world\", \"entities\": [] },\n    \"mediaIds\": [\"m_1\"],\n    \"engagementSummary\": {\n      \"reactions\": { \"like\": 20, \"haha\": 15 },\n      \"totalReactions\": 35,\n      \"commentCount\": 0,\n      \"shareCount\": 0\n    },\n    \"viewerReaction\": null,\n    \"viewerHasShared\": false,\n    \"createdAt\": 1620639583\n  },\n  \"users\": [{ \"id\": \"456\", \"name\": \"John Doe\" }],\n  \"media\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"m_1\",\n      \"src\": \"https://www.example.com/feed-images.jpg\",\n      \"alt\": \"An image alt\",\n      \"width\": 1200,\n      \"height\": 800\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n\n** Optimization **\n\n***** Feed List\n-- Virtualized list: with infinite scrolling, the feed lives in one long-lived scroll container, if user\nscroll down, more post are appended to DOM --> DOM size increase with more complexity\n--> this can cause performance issues in term of DOM size, rendering, browser memory usage\n-- Virtualized list only render post in view port + small overscan window.\nIn practice, Facebook replaces contents off-screen fee posts with spacer <div/> (300px),\nso scroll position is preserved while heavy subtree is removed from the DOM\n====> fewer DOM nodes to render, fewer layout computation to be made.\n====> Simpler empty subtree makes diffing against previous tree cheaper and produce smaller set of real DOM mutation\n\nVirtualization works best when each item has a stable key and the client keeps track of measured heights carefully. \nFeed items can be tricky because text wraps differently across layouts and media loads asynchronously, \nso the virtualization layer may need spacer elements, measurement caches, and recomputation when image dimensions become known. \nThe payoff is not just fewer DOM nodes; it is also less layout thrashing and more predictable scroll performance in long sessions.\n\n2 tradeoff in virtualization\n1) Focused element that scrolls out of window will get unmounted, which loses focus. When user opern\na reaction menu then scroll, the virtualization layer needs to either keep recently focused items mounted,\nor restore focus programatiacaly when they unmounted\n2) In-app search will not work, content outside window will be invisible. \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Response format\n{\n  \"post\": {\n    \"id\": \"124\",\n    \"authorId\": \"456\",\n    \"body\": { \"text\": \"Hello world\", \"entities\": [] },\n    \"mediaIds\": [\"m_1\"],\n    \"engagementSummary\": {\n      \"reactions\": { \"like\": 20, \"haha\": 15 },\n      \"totalReactions\": 35,\n      \"commentCount\": 0,\n      \"shareCount\": 0\n    },\n    \"viewerReaction\": null,\n    \"viewerHasShared\": false,\n    \"createdAt\": 1620639583\n  },\n  \"users\": [{ \"id\": \"456\", \"name\": \"John Doe\" }],\n  \"media\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"m_1\",\n      \"src\": \"https://www.example.com/feed-images.jpg\",\n      \"alt\": \"An image alt\",\n      \"width\": 1200,\n      \"height\": 800\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n\n** Optimization **\n\n***** Feed List\n-- Virtualized list: with infinite scrolling, the feed lives in one long-lived scroll container, if user\nscroll down, more post are appended to DOM --> DOM size increase with more complexity\n--> this can cause performance issues in term of DOM size, rendering, browser memory usage\n-- Virtualized list only render post in view port + small overscan window.\nIn practice, Facebook replaces contents off-screen fee posts with spacer <div/> (300px),\nso scroll position is preserved while heavy subtree is removed from the DOM\n====> fewer DOM nodes to render, fewer layout computation to be made.\n====> Simpler empty subtree makes diffing against previous tree cheaper and produce smaller set of real DOM mutation\n\nVirtualization works best when each item has a stable key and the client keeps track of measured heights carefully. \nFeed items can be tricky because text wraps differently across layouts and media loads asynchronously, \nso the virtualization layer may need spacer elements, measurement caches, and recomputation when image dimensions become known. \nThe payoff is not just fewer DOM nodes; it is also less layout thrashing and more predictable scroll performance in long sessions.\n\n2 tradeoff in virtualization\n1) Focused element that scrolls out of window will get unmounted, which loses focus. When user opern\na reaction menu then scroll, the virtualization layer needs to either keep recently focused items mounted,\nor restore focus programatiacaly when they unmounted\n2) In-app search will not work, content outside window will be invisible. \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "AHL6zYn5rDpIRf3PFcaNu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 218.3115785693729,
      "y": 4707.886180324392,
      "width": 1369.759033203125,
      "height": 925,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b10",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 769545610,
      "version": 2804,
      "versionNonce": 882278714,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781634444077,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "***** Infinite scrolling\n-- A way to reduce waiting time to load for next set of feed post is load feed before user hit the bottom,\nso user never see loading indicator\n1. Listen for \"scroll\" event: throttle scroll event listener on the container/page (via setInterval), \n  obtain the position by Element.getBoundClientRect\n2. Intersection Observer API: monitor when the marker element is entering or exiting another element,\nor intersecting by specific amount\n\n***** User experience\n--- loading indicators : rather than showing spinner, can be better default to skeleton placeholder\nthat resembles thepost structure. ==> reserving expected layout so real content can be swap with minimal to no jump\n--- scrolling position should be preserved if user navigate to another page (feed details) and back to feed. \nThis can be achieved in SPA, if the feed list data is cached within client store along with scroll position.\nwhen user navigate back to the feedpage, since data is already in client, feed list can be read from client store,\nand immediately present on screen the previous scroll position\n--- Stale feeds:\n------ prompt user to refresh or refetch the feed if the last fetched timestamp is too old\n------ when it's refetched, the current feed in store can be entirely removed from memory to free up space\n------ can also fetch newer post in the background and show banner \"new post available\"\n\n****** User with 2 tabs open\n--- reacting in one tab leave other tabs show stales data. Can use BroadcastChannel for this. \n--- Shared storage alone does not notify other tabs of changes. \nWithout a BroadcastChannel or equivalent, \na reaction applied in one tab will sit stale in another tab until the user interacts with it.\n\n\n\n-- Feed posts can come in many different format, and each post would require custom rendering code\n-- Lazy load components depending on data received. (require extra network round-trip to lazy load component, after data is fetched)\n-- For graphQL, Relay couples React component with GraphQL which allow \nReact components to declare exactly which data fields are needed, and Relay will fetch them via GraphQL and provide component with data\n----> data-driven dependencies via @match and @module GraphQL directive, \nThis avoids shipping every renderer upfront and reduces the need for a separate render-time discovery step after the data shape is known.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "***** Infinite scrolling\n-- A way to reduce waiting time to load for next set of feed post is load feed before user hit the bottom,\nso user never see loading indicator\n1. Listen for \"scroll\" event: throttle scroll event listener on the container/page (via setInterval), \n  obtain the position by Element.getBoundClientRect\n2. Intersection Observer API: monitor when the marker element is entering or exiting another element,\nor intersecting by specific amount\n\n***** User experience\n--- loading indicators : rather than showing spinner, can be better default to skeleton placeholder\nthat resembles thepost structure. ==> reserving expected layout so real content can be swap with minimal to no jump\n--- scrolling position should be preserved if user navigate to another page (feed details) and back to feed. \nThis can be achieved in SPA, if the feed list data is cached within client store along with scroll position.\nwhen user navigate back to the feedpage, since data is already in client, feed list can be read from client store,\nand immediately present on screen the previous scroll position\n--- Stale feeds:\n------ prompt user to refresh or refetch the feed if the last fetched timestamp is too old\n------ when it's refetched, the current feed in store can be entirely removed from memory to free up space\n------ can also fetch newer post in the background and show banner \"new post available\"\n\n****** User with 2 tabs open\n--- reacting in one tab leave other tabs show stales data. Can use BroadcastChannel for this. \n--- Shared storage alone does not notify other tabs of changes. \nWithout a BroadcastChannel or equivalent, \na reaction applied in one tab will sit stale in another tab until the user interacts with it.\n\n\n\n-- Feed posts can come in many different format, and each post would require custom rendering code\n-- Lazy load components depending on data received. (require extra network round-trip to lazy load component, after data is fetched)\n-- For graphQL, Relay couples React component with GraphQL which allow \nReact components to declare exactly which data fields are needed, and Relay will fetch them via GraphQL and provide component with data\n----> data-driven dependencies via @match and @module GraphQL directive, \nThis avoids shipping every renderer upfront and reduces the need for a separate render-time discovery step after the data shape is known.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
